    "q-64795": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64795",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-18{y}^{′}+81y=0$ for $y=y(t),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?",
      "worked_solution": "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-18\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+81{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-18\\lambda +81) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-18\\lambda +81 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -9)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =9$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{9t}$ and $t{e}^{9t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $3{e}^{-9t}$ is not a solution.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-64795-a",
