    "q-64796": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64796",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{16}y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?",
      "worked_solution": "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{1}{2}\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\lambda +\\frac{1}{16}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\lambda +\\frac{1}{16} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\lambda +\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-x/4}$ and $x{e}^{-x/4}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $2{e}^{-4x}+3x{e}^{-x/4}$ is not a solution.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-64796-a",
